      "raw_response": "REFUTED\n\nThe abstract from the study titled \"Effect of physical activity on cognitive function in older adults at risk for Alzheimer's disease: a randomized trial.\" suggests that a 24-week physical activity intervention provided a modest improvement in cognition over an 18-month follow-up period for adults with subjective memory impairment. It states that participants in the intervention group improved in ADAS-Cog scores at both the end of the intervention and at 18 months, whereas those in the usual care group deteriorated. This contradicts the claim that physical activity does not improve cognitive function in individuals with Alzheimer's. (Source: Journal of the International Neuropsychological Society, 2009)\n\nANSWER: The claim is refuted by the study.",
